            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

We had identified many locations of excess air leakage (heat loss) from your home:



** The section of ceiling where the front &amp; back halves of the original house come together is not sealed in the attic. We could feel the extensive air leakage (during the blower door test) by standing in the door entrances to each bedroom.



** The older windows and doors, as well as exterior wall plugs &amp; switches



** The bathroom exhaust fan



** The attic hatch can be sealed with a "removable" caulking, such as Draft Attack



** The rear addition has extensive air leakage through the walls and around door &amp; windows



Savings: The software models a modest 25% reduction in overall air leakage (although a minimum 50% reduction is what you should try to achieve), that could potentially save $ 381 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.4 GJ, and qualify for $ 750 in rebates. For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000. I personally hope you achieve &gt;50% and receive the $1,000 rebate.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of the original house are 2x4@16" with R12 insulation and the exterior walls of the laundry room &amp; rear addition are 2x6@16" with R20 insulation. We talked about your interest in having a local contractor add a minimum R10 of rigid foam insulation to the exterior of all the walls. Please remember there are exterior walls of the house &amp; the laundry room that can be seen from inside the garage and these will require more insulation as well !!



Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover some of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding.



I did mention that our DHERP will be expiring on March 31, 2024, so there is really only this summer &amp; next summer for you to complete this project .......... if you want to qualify through this program.



For your house, I modeled the adding another R10 to 100% of the exterior walls of the house, however you can add more (R15 or R20) if you want as your house has lots of soffit overhang, and the additional insulation will qualify you for a higher grant level .......... that should pay for the cost of the additional insulation.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 635 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 20.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). Adding R15 would increase the rebate to $5,000 and adding R20 would increase it to $8,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $4,290 is available for adding R10 insulation to 100% of the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <windows>

All of the windows in your house are older aluminum or wood windows. There is one double pane vinyl window, however you will likely want to update this one as well, as you update the other 8 windows. I recommend you install ENERGY STAR?????? qualified, full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing all 9 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 768 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 24.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,250 in rebates (9 windows max @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</windows>
                <doors>

Both exterior doors (the front entrance of the original house &amp; the rear, left entrance of the rear addition) are older &amp; worn, allowing much air leakage heat loss. You mentioned that you are considering upgrading both exterior doors with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. In addition, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m??????K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.



Savings:

By replacing these 2 entry door systems with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 12 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $325.00 in rebates (2 door units @ $162.50 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 142 litre electric hot water tank. As electricity is quite expensive in Fort Smith and you essentially have a very large "electric kettle", we discussed an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.95 EF, could potentially save $ 634 per year in energy costs,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 82.1% efficient, oil furnace. I have modeled an option for you to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 96% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 103 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 31.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you do not complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you could still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler that is a minimum 97% AFUE. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $12.000 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the wall insulation

     - $2,250 for upgrading 9 windows with eligible ESTAR units

     - $3,000 for an eligible furnace upgrade

     - $   750 for a 20% - 30% air leakage reduction ($1,000 if you achieve &gt;30%)

     - $3,000 Bonus if you achieve a 30% - 40% total energy reduction 



For more information, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



????????? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

????????? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

????????? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

????????? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

????????? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

????????? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, the 1 bathroom fan and the clothes dryer), indicated no pressure drop as there is currently too much air leakage from your home. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is chimney vented and is susceptible to back-drafting if the house were too air tight (no current concern of this).



You are planning to install a wood or pellet stove in the rear addition. Please ensure a dedicated &amp; sealed outside combustion air supply pipe is provided directly to the wood or pellet stove.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood (does not vent to the outside)

        - 1 bathroom fan

        - The clothes dryer



If you replace any of the bathroom fans (or the principle fan), choose ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. For a new bathroom fan, consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Heat Distribution



The current furnace was designed to heat the original house and likely has adequate capacity to heat the laundry room addition. However, the heat duct supply system is not set up to effectively heat the rear addition. You mentioned that during this past winter, you have been unable to get the house temperature above 60 F.



The rear addition used to have a wood stove installed, however it has failed &amp; has been removed. You are trying to have a wood pellet stove installed, however are having troubles finding a qualified contractor to install &amp; certify it. In the interim, the "heat drain" on the house (from that rear addition) is significant &amp; the furnace cannot keep up. You have therefore had a temporary wall &amp; door installed to exclude that area from the rest of the house.



We also identified a few other things that are affecting the ability of the furnace to heat your house:



        - The return air to the furnace is through the wall into the front entrance area. You normally have the door from the entrance into the rest of the house closed, which prevents the furnace from drawing cold air from the rest of the house. A qualified heating contractor should have a closer look at the return air design of your furnace to see if there is a better setup.

        - Your house is a "double wide" design, where the front &amp; back sections were brought separately &amp; assembled onsite. It appears they never sealed the joint in the attic where the 2 sections come together, as you &amp; I could feel ALOT of air leakage coming through the "joint" - as experienced when standing in many of the bedroom entrance door locations. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Installing a Pellet or Wood Stove in the Rear Addition



Rebates:

If you purchase &amp; install a new, eligible wood or pellet stove can qualify for 50% of the purchase price up to $ 1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



